  "body": "Found while widening the built-in Lathe's X floor for #630 (PR #640).\n\n**Today:** the machine travel check compares each move's world X with the lathe's X band. The turning kernels put their radius on the **Y** meridian (a cached facing path is (0, y, z)), so on those paths the X band never sees the turning radius, whether nose centre or control point. A lathe X over-travel along the Y meridian is never judged by the stroke check. (Thread arcs in XY *are* seen, which is how #631 surfaced.) It also judges the cache (nose centre), not the posted control point that #640 now writes.\n\n**Expected:** for lathe machines, the stroke check judges the **posted** position: the radius on the path's meridian (X or Y, the same way `nose_tip::control_point_path` reads it), shifted to the control point, against the X band; Z against the Z band. An over-travel is refused by name.\n\n**Done means:** a facing path on the Y meridian that runs past the X floor is refused by name. A control point (not the nose centre) at the band edge decides it. Existing lathe goldens and suites stay green.\n\nRelated: #630/#640 (control point), #631/#643 (spindle-axis arcs).",
